What issues did they have?

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:54.843
And how did we help
them solve those issues?

00:00:54.843 --> 00:00:57.260
After that, we're going to
review some of the architecture

00:00:57.260 --> 00:00:57.890
trends.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.960
There's really two
different use case,

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.770
two different types of projects
that we're going to review.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:02.930
So we're going to look at
the architecture trends

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.305
amongst those types of projects.

00:01:04.305 --> 00:01:05.930
We're going to hand
it off to Ray then.

00:01:05.930 --> 00:01:07.930
He's going to review some
of the common services

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.848
that we're using on
these rapid prototypes.

00:01:09.848 --> 00:01:11.390
Before I move on
here, though, I just

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.090
want to get a quick idea of
where everyone in the audience

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:15.980
is at with GCP.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:22.040
So, I guess, the first question,
who knows what GCP stands for?

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.490
OK, good.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:24.960
We're off to a good start.

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.740
So who's using GCP in
production right now,

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:30.700
or you're well on your way?

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.090
OK, cool.

00:01:32.090 --> 00:01:33.240
Good.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.690
How about if you're
evaluating GCP?

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.700
Maybe you're on your way,
but still kind of new.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:40.150
Couple of hands.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:42.130
All right, cool.

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:42.818
Good.

00:01:42.818 --> 00:01:43.860
Got a mix of people here.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.470
So that's good.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:47.100
Last question.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.710
So some of you might know 12
animals of the lunar calendar.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.310
I don't know if anyone's
familiar with that here.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.070
How many of you
know what year 2019

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.970
is, what animal it represents?

00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.140
The year of the pig.

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.200
So once we learned that,
coming from Minnesota,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.509
we couldn't pass up the
opportunity to talk about one

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.088
of our favorite clients and
their internet-connected pigs,

00:02:12.088 --> 00:02:13.880
a project that we more
affectionately refer

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:15.215
to as the Internet of Swine.

00:02:18.809 --> 00:02:21.270
So you might be wondering,
why would someone

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:23.313
want to connect their
pigs to the internet?

00:02:23.313 --> 00:02:24.480
It seems a little bit weird.

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.920
We thought it was a
little bit strange

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.087
when we first were approached
with this opportunity.

00:02:28.087 --> 00:02:30.390
But see, our client
is an ag tech company.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:33.060
And they saw an
opportunity to help

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.235
farms improve their efficiency
and increase revenue.

00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:37.860
They knew that they
spend a lot of time

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:41.940
monitoring the health of pigs
for reproductive reasons.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:43.777
And they're also very
prone to get sick.

00:02:43.777 --> 00:02:45.360
So, if they could
track their activity

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.670
and build a predictive
data model around it,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:51.720
they could help the
farmhands save time.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.560
So they had a hardware
team that managed

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.110
the embedded development.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:01.805
So I'm going to review
the hardware here.

00:03:01.805 --> 00:03:03.180
They brought us
in to really help

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.330
with the cloud infrastructure.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.670
So we're going to look at
the app architecture right

00:03:05.670 --> 00:03:06.428
after this.

00:03:06.428 --> 00:03:08.220
But just so you understand
how the data was

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:11.460
getting to the cloud, let's
take a look at the hardware.

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.265
So first we've got the ear tag.

00:03:14.265 --> 00:03:16.860
The ear tag was attached
directly to the pig.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.723
It had an accelerometer in it.

00:03:18.723 --> 00:03:20.640
And that accelerometer
tracked their movements

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.010
so that they could tell if
they were sitting, standing,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.975
laying down, all of the above.

00:03:25.975 --> 00:03:27.850
It would also collect
the beacon information,

00:03:27.850 --> 00:03:29.860
which is the middle there.

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:31.950
So the beacons were placed
in the feeding troughs

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.290
and in the water reservoirs.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.420
And they would emit a signal.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.660
And the ear tags would
measure that RSSI.

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.460
And if it got close
enough, it would then

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.830
collect the beacon
information and send it up

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:46.800
to the Raspberry Pi, which
is the third item there

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.340
on the right.

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:50.490
That was just hung in
the rafters of the barns,

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.640
connected with the ear
tags via Bluetooth,

00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.010
and then acted as a
bridge up to the cloud.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.700
So now that you understand how
the data was getting there,

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.590
let's look at what we did
once it was in the cloud.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.670
So as mentioned,
got the pig here

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:08.610
representing the ear tags
connecting to the Raspberry Pi

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:10.570
with Bluetooth.

00:04:10.570 --> 00:04:13.590
The Raspberry Pi was then
transmitting the data

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.980
into the cloud using
an MQTT bridge.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.279
So initially that was with
RabbitMQ and a custom-built IoT

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:23.600
adapter that Agosto developed.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.050
Nowadays, that's
replaced with IoT Core.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.880
So once the event data is landed
in Pub/Sub, or in the cloud--

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.290
excuse me--

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.210
we throw it into
a Pub/Sub topic,

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.440
which can then be
consumed by GKE,

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.490
Kubernetes Engine in this case.

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:41.860
Kubernetes then did
some data transformation

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:45.287
and wrote it to BigQuery for
some additional analytics

00:04:45.287 --> 00:04:46.870
and their data team
to be able to pull

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:48.940
out and do some
visualization tools

00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:51.350
and build their data models.

00:04:51.350 --> 00:04:52.870
You also see Datastore up there.

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.810
That was just a simple
backup storage in case

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.090
there was any errors
writing it to BigQuery.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.310
We stored the raw event
data in Datastore.

00:05:04.688 --> 00:05:06.480
The last thing here
that I want to touch on

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:07.890
is just the power of Kubernetes.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.848
I'm sure you guys have
heard about it all NEXT.

00:05:09.848 --> 00:05:12.120
But once we got
this up and running,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:14.820
we really found out quickly
that one pod was not

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.920
going to be enough
to handle hundreds

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.140
of devices coming in
per second from all

00:05:19.140 --> 00:05:20.650
of the pigs in the barn.

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:22.890
So we were able to make
a quick config update,

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.140
scale it up to three pods--

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:27.300
that was just for
one deployment--

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.700
to be able to spread out the
workload across all those pods.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.390
Ultimately, this is kind of
what their visualization started

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:37.650
to look like.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:40.745
They pulled it out and were able
to tell when they were sitting

00:05:40.745 --> 00:05:42.120
and both when they
were drinking,

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:44.980
amongst a wide breadth
of other data models

00:05:44.980 --> 00:05:46.980
that they were able to
successfully build around

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:50.610
reproductive and, like,
if they're nesting,

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.220
if they're ready to give
birth, things like that.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.370
So in just over two weeks,
they were able to get up

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.170
and running successfully
from the barn

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.580
all the way to
the cloud in order

00:05:59.580 --> 00:06:00.820
to build their data models.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.310
So it's pretty quick.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.150
And we were able
to do it leveraging

00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:06.910
those cloud native services.

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:11.290
So that's just one
example of IoT.

00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:13.050
We're going to look
at another one here.

00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:16.580
It's a little bit different
than monitoring pigs.

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:19.350
So this is a manufacturing
and technology company.

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.610
And this manufacturing
and technology company,

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.200
they have a series of devices
that help organizations

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.160
of all shapes and sizes manage
and monitor their inventory.

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.780
One example is
actually the badge.

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:33.510
They have some printers.

00:06:33.510 --> 00:06:37.470
So they help print the badges
on your NEXT certificate,

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:42.730
or your NEXT badge this week.

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:46.440
So in order to help them
manage that inventory,

00:06:46.440 --> 00:06:48.750
they wanted a
scalable IT solution.

00:06:48.750 --> 00:06:51.090
Again, they had the
manufacturing down

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:53.220
and their own development
teams that were managing

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:55.400
the firmware on this hardware.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.730
But they needed something
scalable and in the cloud.

00:06:57.730 --> 00:07:01.440
So here we come with GCP.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.500
Over just three weeks, we were
able to get this architecture

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.920
up and running.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.180
It was initial PoC in
which, once we were actually

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.460
wrapped with this,
they selected GCP

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:13.230
as their cloud
provider of choice

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:17.160
because we were able to prove
out that it can scale rapidly

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:20.590
and to the size that they need.

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:22.440
So again, very similar
to the pigs here,

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:24.930
we've got the device on the
left-hand side communicating

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.930
with IoT Core via
an MQTT bridge.

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:33.000
From that MQTT bridge,
we landed in Pub/Sub.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.640
Instead of GKE here,
we have it being

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:37.430
consumed by a Cloud Function.

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:38.930
So it's just another
compute option.

00:07:38.930 --> 00:07:41.010
I'm sure you guys have
heard Cloud Functions.

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:45.240
And then landing it in
BigQuery for a data warehouse.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.130
After they selected
GCP, we added

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:48.490
some additional functionality.

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.280
So this isn't within
the three weeks,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:52.320
this extra functionality.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:55.860
But you can see it's kind of
a daisy chain, Pub/Sub, Cloud

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:57.360
Functions.

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:59.580
And what it's essentially
doing is doing a lookup

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:01.590
into Firestore, which is
where they were storing

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:04.720
some additional ancillary data.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:08.850
And if there was filter related
to that event that came in,

00:08:08.850 --> 00:08:11.550
they would land it in a Cloud
Function, apply those filters.

00:08:11.550 --> 00:08:14.190
If the filters were
passed, then he'd

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:15.780
throw it in another
Pub/Sub queue

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:18.760
to then eventually
notify this webhook.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.010
And the webhooks were dynamic.

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:23.550
So it could be an infinite
number of webhooks.

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:27.060
One example our customer used to
demonstrate to their customers

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.850
was changing a Philips
hue light bulb color.

00:08:30.850 --> 00:08:35.289
So from red to green
to blue to you name it.

00:08:35.289 --> 00:08:39.570
So that's two examples
here of two different IoT

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:41.490
architectures.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:43.809
Pretty different use cases.

00:08:43.809 --> 00:08:46.840
So you might be wondering,
how does this help you?

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:49.350
And if you don't have
an IoT architecture,

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:51.250
it might not directly.

00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:54.180
But when you start to strip away
some of the specific tools that

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:56.052
are being used and you
genericize these app

00:08:56.052 --> 00:08:58.260
architecture, you can start
to see some of the trends

00:08:58.260 --> 00:08:59.510
that we're talking about here.

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:03.250
So moving forward here, back
to the Internet of Swine,

00:09:03.250 --> 00:09:06.170
we've got IoT Core,
and you've got Pub/Sub.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:09.360
So IoT Core and Pub/Sub are
acting as your ingest and event

00:09:09.360 --> 00:09:10.590
queue.

00:09:10.590 --> 00:09:13.290
From there, you've got it
landing in the Compute.

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:15.930
And then BigQuery is for
analytics, data storage,

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:18.920
your backup storage.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:22.240
If we compare this to the
inventory and operations,

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:24.330
got IoT Core and Pub/Sub.

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.450
They're acting as your
ingest and event queue.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:32.135
Cloud Functions is your compute,
and BigQuery is your analytics.

00:09:32.135 --> 00:09:33.010
Looks pretty similar.

00:09:38.450 --> 00:09:40.510
So, when you start
to map that out,

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:43.807
you can draw a generic
diagram of this nature.

00:09:43.807 --> 00:09:45.640
So you've got your IoT
devices, whether it's

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:49.210
a light bulb, a speaker, a
tablet, a gateway device,

00:09:49.210 --> 00:09:51.090
communicating to the cloud.

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:54.730
Could be MQTT, could be HTTP.

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:56.260
All of the above work.

00:09:56.260 --> 00:10:02.860
Lands in, typically, IoT Core,
to then be consumed by Pub/Sub.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.298
Once it's in Pub/Sub,
it can be consumed

00:10:05.298 --> 00:10:07.840
by any of those compute options,
Cloud Functions, App Engine,

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:10.240
Compute Engine, Kubernetes.

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.270
And then land it in
storage and both analytics.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:15.730
Usually for analytics,
we're seeing BigQuery.

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:16.848
It's a very powerful tool.

00:10:16.848 --> 00:10:18.890
A lot of customers want
to get their hands on it.

00:10:22.610 --> 00:10:25.940
Moving on to our
next set of projects,

00:10:25.940 --> 00:10:28.052
this is analytics and
ETL-style projects.

00:10:28.052 --> 00:10:29.510
So we're going to
talk a little bit

00:10:29.510 --> 00:10:32.510
about some of the trends
we're seeing among those.

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:34.400
So the first ETL
customer, they come to us

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.777
as a Fortune 500 retailer.

00:10:36.777 --> 00:10:38.360
They have their
online shopping model,

00:10:38.360 --> 00:10:40.690
like all Fortune
500 retailers do.

00:10:40.690 --> 00:10:42.620
They have an option
to ship from store.

00:10:42.620 --> 00:10:44.537
So their customers, when
they're checking out,

00:10:44.537 --> 00:10:45.620
they can ship from store.

00:10:45.620 --> 00:10:47.708
And when they're
shipping from store,

00:10:47.708 --> 00:10:49.250
they can get it at
an expedited rate.

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.500
But the tough part
for our customer,

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:54.740
they didn't have a way of
tracking whether that order

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:56.280
remained profitable for them.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:02.330
So they came to us with the
ask of helping them define,

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:04.580
how can we determine if
these ship from store orders

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:05.407
are profitable?

00:11:05.407 --> 00:11:06.740
So this was a little bit longer.

00:11:06.740 --> 00:11:10.250
You can say it was three months
instead of in the weeks range

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.380
that some of the
IoT projects were.

00:11:12.380 --> 00:11:14.500
It's actually because
our data scientists,

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:18.920
they took a data dump of all of
their orders for a year or two,

00:11:18.920 --> 00:11:22.760
used Datalab to essentially
analyze all the data

00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:26.270
and build an algorithm to help
determine if it was profitable.

00:11:26.270 --> 00:11:29.570
So once we were able to
propose that to the client,

00:11:29.570 --> 00:11:31.010
the client approved it.

00:11:31.010 --> 00:11:34.310
And we were able to get moving
on the actual architecture

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:36.360
itself.

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:40.184
So let's take a look at what
that architecture looked like.

00:11:40.184 --> 00:11:44.370
So as I mentioned, you've got
the online ordering system.

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.770
So as soon as the
customer placed an order,

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:50.780
they landed it on
a Kafka server.

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:53.510
From there, we used
a Pub/Sub connector

00:11:53.510 --> 00:11:54.567
to land it in Pub/Sub.

00:11:54.567 --> 00:11:56.150
And the reason that
we didn't bring it

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:58.520
straight to Dataflow,
under certain environments

00:11:58.520 --> 00:11:59.810
you certainly could do that.

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:03.440
This specific customer
had a firewall

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.280
that we just couldn't bypass.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:09.260
So we landed it in
Pub/Sub with the connector

00:12:09.260 --> 00:12:11.030
as a way to get
it into Dataflow.

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:16.850
So from there, Pub/Sub consumed
the messages off the topic,

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:18.260
ran its ETL job--

00:12:18.260 --> 00:12:20.900
in this case, the algorithm
that our data scientists

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:24.440
had created in
the initial phase.

00:12:24.440 --> 00:12:27.740
And then we land it in BigQuery
for their analytics team.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:30.065
And their business users
then consumed it via Tableau.

00:12:33.230 --> 00:12:36.140
So after about three
months, this retailer

00:12:36.140 --> 00:12:39.740
went from not being able
to determine whether or not

00:12:39.740 --> 00:12:42.950
an item was profitable
to being able to turn off

00:12:42.950 --> 00:12:46.940
the ship from store item
option on their online shopping

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:48.830
store for those items.

00:12:52.040 --> 00:12:54.560
Next company here
for ETL we're going

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:55.880
to talk a little bit about.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:12:57.550
It's a medical device company.

00:12:57.550 --> 00:13:00.230
They're located in
Minneapolis, Minnesota.

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:02.930
They have a companion
app that they've

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:04.910
published for their
medical device,

00:13:04.910 --> 00:13:07.490
helps the users
configure and monitor

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:09.093
the activity on their device.

00:13:09.093 --> 00:13:11.510
And so they actually built
this with Firebase to get it up

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:13.020
and running quickly.

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:16.370
They use Firestore and Cloud
Storage on the back end.

00:13:16.370 --> 00:13:20.300
And as their research and
development team started to see

00:13:20.300 --> 00:13:23.180
the data coming in from the
users-- all the usage data,

00:13:23.180 --> 00:13:24.380
diagnostics--

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:27.290
they realized that there was
a big opportunity for them

00:13:27.290 --> 00:13:29.930
to improve their users'
experience by looking

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.660
at that data.

00:13:32.660 --> 00:13:35.120
What they ended up
doing was coming to us

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:39.080
because, in the medical
industry, there's HIPAA.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:44.000
And your R&amp;D team can't just
get their hands on all that PHI.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.290
That's not good.

00:13:45.290 --> 00:13:49.160
So they came to us to build
them a sanitized pipeline

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:51.500
that their R&amp;D team could
then build models off

00:13:51.500 --> 00:13:55.950
to improve their firmware
on these devices.

00:13:55.950 --> 00:14:00.067
So after doing a review of
their app architecture--

00:14:00.067 --> 00:14:02.150
as I mentioned, Firestore
and Cloud Storage really

00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:03.810
helped us move this along.

00:14:03.810 --> 00:14:05.660
Four weeks.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:08.660
Architecture looked a little
bit something like that.

00:14:08.660 --> 00:14:11.990
Again, you've got
your medical device.

00:14:11.990 --> 00:14:14.780
It landed-- the app
then wrote to Firestore.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:18.500
So as soon as it wrote
the Firestore document,

00:14:18.500 --> 00:14:22.850
we put an on right Cloud
Function trigger on that data

00:14:22.850 --> 00:14:24.830
element type in Firestore.

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:27.530
So if that changed, whether
it was a create, an update,

00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:30.470
a delete event, really
anything, it would then

00:14:30.470 --> 00:14:33.680
trigger this Cloud Function, do
a lookup in the Cloud Storage

00:14:33.680 --> 00:14:35.745
to find any related data.

00:14:35.745 --> 00:14:37.370
And then if it found
that related data,

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:40.100
it would land a
message on Pub/Sub.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:42.050
From here, it's all the same.

00:14:42.050 --> 00:14:43.490
So it lands into--

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:46.040
Dataflow consumes that
message from Pub/Sub,

00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:48.230
performs its ETL--

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.980
in this case, removing all
PHI and anonymizing the data

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:54.500
and then landing it in BigQuery
for their analytics team

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.000
to do with what they will.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:00.458
Again, as we look at
these two ETL projects,

00:15:00.458 --> 00:15:02.500
hopefully you can start
to see some of the trends

00:15:02.500 --> 00:15:06.400
that you saw with
the IoT projects.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:09.550
With the online
ordering system, Kafka,

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:11.410
it acted as the event trigger.

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:13.870
This is a streaming
job, so any event

00:15:13.870 --> 00:15:16.900
trigger that then landed
in Pub/Sub for the message

00:15:16.900 --> 00:15:20.590
queue, that then landed
in Dataflow for ETL,

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:22.300
and finally BigQuery
for analytics.

00:15:24.980 --> 00:15:27.620
The research and data models.

00:15:27.620 --> 00:15:29.570
That Firestore and the
Cloud Function that

00:15:29.570 --> 00:15:31.803
acted as the event trigger.

00:15:31.803 --> 00:15:33.470
From there, everything's
the same again.

00:15:33.470 --> 00:15:35.060
Message queue is Pub/Sub.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:36.860
Dataflow is your ETL tool.

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.798
BigQuery is for analytics.

00:15:38.798 --> 00:15:41.090
So again, that might look a
little something like this.

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:44.660
You have your event trigger,
whatever that might be.

00:15:44.660 --> 00:15:46.430
Could be a Cloud Function.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:48.770
Could be the Kafka server.

00:15:48.770 --> 00:15:50.730
Could be anything, really.

00:15:50.730 --> 00:15:52.220
Then you land it in Pub/Sub.

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:53.720
That's your messaging queue.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:56.265
From there, you've got your
ETL pipeline and analytics.

00:15:56.265 --> 00:15:58.640
Obviously, there's some other
additional analytic options

00:15:58.640 --> 00:15:59.880
for analytics.

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:02.240
But as I mentioned
earlier, BigQuery

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:06.700
seems to be the tool of
choice amongst our customers.

00:16:06.700 --> 00:16:07.980
So one quick note up here.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:09.860
You'll see I also put
all the compute options

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.230
under ETL pipeline.

00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:15.620
If you think back to our
IoT projects, both of those

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:19.190
were also doing ETL with
Cloud Functions and Kub.

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:21.860
So you can roll your own.

00:16:21.860 --> 00:16:23.660
Dataflow is a great tool.

00:16:23.660 --> 00:16:25.460
It fits your use case, for one.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:27.582
And two, if you're trying
to get stood up quickly.

00:16:27.582 --> 00:16:29.540
But we always have the
option to roll your own.

00:16:29.540 --> 00:16:33.650
And the GCP compute services
are a great option for that.

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:37.520
So that's a lot of architecture.

00:16:37.520 --> 00:16:39.890
We went through
that kind of fast.

00:16:39.890 --> 00:16:41.900
What is this-- well,
what's the point of it all?

00:16:41.900 --> 00:16:44.192
So what we're trying
to say is there's

00:16:44.192 --> 00:16:46.400
a lot of ways to accomplish
what you're trying to do.

00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:48.350
And everyone has
a unique problem.

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:50.810
You all have your own
industry, your own company.

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:53.413
You're facing your
unique issues.

00:16:53.413 --> 00:16:54.830
And we don't want
you to walk away

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:57.650
thinking that these are the
only ways to solve the problem,

00:16:57.650 --> 00:16:59.870
because there's an
infinite number of ways

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:02.460
that you can go home
and solve your issues.

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:05.480
But what we're trying to
say is that a unique problem

00:17:05.480 --> 00:17:08.780
doesn't need to equal
a unique solution.

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:13.250
You saw two examples of two
different companies that

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:14.970
had very different use cases.

00:17:14.970 --> 00:17:18.000
But they benefited from the
same underlying architecture.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.900
So reference architectures
are your friends.

00:17:20.900 --> 00:17:22.849
There's no shame in using it.

00:17:22.849 --> 00:17:25.069
It is tried and tested.

00:17:25.069 --> 00:17:29.340
And quite frankly, there is no
extra credit for originality.

00:17:29.340 --> 00:17:31.850
So you can save both your
organization time and money.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:35.770
All right.

00:17:35.770 --> 00:17:38.211
So switching gears a little bit.

00:17:38.211 --> 00:17:43.650
You might get home from
NEXT, be ready to start down

00:17:43.650 --> 00:17:46.080
this journey, a
new project, come

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:48.752
across a list that looks a
little something like this,

00:17:48.752 --> 00:17:50.460
and then close your
laptop because you're

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:52.560
a little bit overwhelmed.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:55.120
So to help curb that
overwhelming feeling,

00:17:55.120 --> 00:17:56.440
pass it off to Ray over here.

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:58.398
He's going to talk about
some of these services

00:17:58.398 --> 00:17:59.754
that we see most frequently.

00:17:59.754 --> 00:18:03.093
[APPLAUSE]

00:18:05.707 --> 00:18:06.790
RAY PITMON: Hey, everyone.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:07.290
I'm Ray.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:13.500
So that's a big list.

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:17.313
50-plus platform services, APIs.

00:18:17.313 --> 00:18:18.730
I think I've got
about 10 minutes.

00:18:18.730 --> 00:18:22.640
So we're going to go into a
deep dive, all 50 of them.

00:18:22.640 --> 00:18:24.090
Don't worry, I'll talk fast.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.720
You guys ready?

00:18:25.720 --> 00:18:26.850
I'm just kidding.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:29.190
But we are going to discuss
a few of the favorites

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:32.437
that I have and stuff
we use for prototyping.

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:38.260
First thing I'm going to
start with is IoT Core.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:44.710
It's Google's
managed IoT platform.

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:46.240
Why do I like IoT Core?

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:47.410
Well, it's server-less.

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:53.620
So a few slides back, Patrick
showed the IoT adapter

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:55.600
that we made in RabbitMQ.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:57.880
That all ran on a
virtual machine.

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:03.310
Required patching
management configuration.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.140
Plus, building a
rabbit cluster that

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:08.780
can handle a million connected
devices is not trivial.

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.080
So IoT Core will just scale up.

00:19:11.080 --> 00:19:14.570
You can connect as many
devices as you want to it.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.830
So let's talk about some of
the features of IoT Core.

00:19:17.830 --> 00:19:22.330
So with IoT Core, it supports
device state configuration

00:19:22.330 --> 00:19:25.740
and authentication.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:30.840
For communication with IoT
Core, you can use MQTT and HTTP.

00:19:30.840 --> 00:19:33.880
And we all know about HTTP.

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:35.470
Let's talk about
MQTT for a minute.

00:19:35.470 --> 00:19:39.580
So MQTT is a light rate
messaging protocol.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:42.550
Runs on TCP.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:45.310
One of the differences
between HTTP and MQTT

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:49.730
is that an MQTT device will
typically make a connection

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:51.610
and hold it open to the server.

00:19:51.610 --> 00:19:55.450
So HTTP, usually, a
device will connect,

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:59.860
send a bunch of headers,
negotiate encryption,

00:19:59.860 --> 00:20:01.270
and then disconnect.

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:04.240
With MQTT, it holds a connection
open for as long as it can.

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.220
Could be hours.

00:20:06.220 --> 00:20:08.450
By doing so, you don't
have all that overhead.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:10.540
So it uses a lot less
data, especially when

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:13.810
you're talking about a
device that maybe you're

00:20:13.810 --> 00:20:15.660
paying for cell data on.

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:21.670
And plus, when you have a
device connected via MQTT,

00:20:21.670 --> 00:20:23.530
you can push config to it.

00:20:23.530 --> 00:20:28.180
So a device is already
subscribed to configuration

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:29.290
or command topics.

00:20:29.290 --> 00:20:34.360
And so what you do is you use
an API called int IoT Core,

00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:36.760
and it'll push config
directly to the device.

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:38.343
You don't have to
wait for that device

00:20:38.343 --> 00:20:41.285
to make it a request
to pull data.

00:20:41.285 --> 00:20:44.890
And IoT Core is really, its core
purpose is a protocol bridge.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:49.142
So when you connect IoT
Core, a device sends data,

00:20:49.142 --> 00:20:50.350
it really ends up in Pub/Sub.

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:57.350
So Pub/Sub, it's a managed
real-time messaging service

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:00.205
for GCP.

00:21:00.205 --> 00:21:06.607
So an IoT Core device sends
data and ends up in Pub/Sub.

00:21:06.607 --> 00:21:08.190
And it's consumed
by your application.

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:11.440
Your application is a
subscriber in Pub/Sub.

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:15.030
So, as I said,
messages are published.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:16.602
So you publish to
a topic in Pub/Sub.

00:21:16.602 --> 00:21:18.560
And again, it doesn't
have to be just IoT Core.

00:21:18.560 --> 00:21:22.040
But you can use Pub/Sub
for sending messages

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:23.990
from machine to machine.

00:21:23.990 --> 00:21:26.150
So you'd be a publisher
and publish to a topic.

00:21:26.150 --> 00:21:27.560
And then you'd have subscribers.

00:21:27.560 --> 00:21:28.940
So you have multiple
applications

00:21:28.940 --> 00:21:31.080
that could subscribe
to the same topic.

00:21:31.080 --> 00:21:34.355
So you can fan out the data,
send it multiple places.

00:21:37.027 --> 00:21:38.360
There are other uses of Pub/Sub.

00:21:38.360 --> 00:21:41.640
So we use Pub/Sub commonly
to distribute work.

00:21:41.640 --> 00:21:43.760
So in one of the other
examples that Patrick had,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:48.110
he showed Cloud Functions,
daisy-chaining via Pub/Sub.

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:50.420
So you could have one Cloud
Function has a lot of work

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:50.920
to do.

00:21:50.920 --> 00:21:53.690
What it can do is it can
publish that to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:55.880
You can have many Cloud
Functions subscribed,

00:21:55.880 --> 00:21:57.030
all doing the same thing.

00:21:57.030 --> 00:21:58.880
So you end up
scaling horizontally

00:21:58.880 --> 00:22:00.050
without doing a lot of work.

00:22:07.150 --> 00:22:10.090
So a couple of-- some of the
features important to Pub/Sub

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:12.140
is it's a durable message queue.

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:13.990
So if a message makes
it into Pub/Sub,

00:22:13.990 --> 00:22:16.690
it's going to stay there
until you pull it out.

00:22:16.690 --> 00:22:19.590
Or, I think it's seven days, it
will eventually throw it away.

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:21.890
It has fan in, fan out support.

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:24.400
So as you can see, you can
have multiple publishers

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:25.810
publishing the same topic.

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:29.850
You can have multiple
subscribers of the same topic.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:31.440
It's easy to integrate.

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:35.070
There are client libraries
for just about every language.

00:22:35.070 --> 00:22:36.250
It's very performant.

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:38.300
And as I said, it's
guaranteed delivery.

00:22:38.300 --> 00:22:40.510
So when a message
is published into--

00:22:40.510 --> 00:22:43.120
when a message is
pushed into Pub/Sub,

00:22:43.120 --> 00:22:44.470
it's waiting for a subscriber.

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:46.480
A subscriber pulls
that message, it

00:22:46.480 --> 00:22:48.510
has to acknowledge that
it got the message.

00:22:48.510 --> 00:22:51.100
Otherwise, Pub/Sub will
put it back in the queue

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:53.270
and wait for someone
else to grab it.

00:22:53.270 --> 00:22:55.610
Are a few caveats
to using Pub/Sub.

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:57.370
The message order
isn't guaranteed.

00:22:57.370 --> 00:22:59.620
So if order is important,
you'll have to sort that out

00:22:59.620 --> 00:23:02.650
in your subscriber side.

00:23:02.650 --> 00:23:05.800
Pub/Sub supports at
least once delivery.

00:23:05.800 --> 00:23:07.570
What that means is
that it's guaranteed

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:08.510
to deliver it at least once.

00:23:08.510 --> 00:23:10.135
But it could deliver
it more than once.

00:23:10.135 --> 00:23:12.850
So you could get duplicates.

00:23:12.850 --> 00:23:15.970
You're also limited to 10,000
topics and 10,000 subscriptions

00:23:15.970 --> 00:23:17.860
per GCP project.

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:20.650
So because of that,
that's one reason

00:23:20.650 --> 00:23:24.000
you wouldn't have an end device
publish directly to Pub/Sub.

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:27.460
You have more than 10,000
devices publishing 10,000

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:29.300
topics, you don't do that.

00:23:29.300 --> 00:23:32.380
So that's what you
use IoT Core for.

00:23:37.012 --> 00:23:39.220
Next thing I'm going to talk
about is Cloud Function.

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:43.430
So we've been using
Cloud Functions a lot.

00:23:43.430 --> 00:23:45.640
Cloud Functions,
obviously they're

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:50.020
server-less, easy to deploy.

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:52.760
You can trigger Cloud Functions
a couple of different ways.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.250
So if you need an HTTP request.

00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:59.690
So I think about just a typical
web service, make a request in.

00:23:59.690 --> 00:24:01.630
You could also trigger
a Cloud Function

00:24:01.630 --> 00:24:03.590
from a file dropped
in Cloud Storage.

00:24:03.590 --> 00:24:07.030
So we'll have an
ETL pipeline that

00:24:07.030 --> 00:24:09.410
might have a Cloud Function
watching a Cloud Storage

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:09.910
bucket.

00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:12.852
File shows up, it's
automatically triggered.

00:24:12.852 --> 00:24:15.060
It knows about the file in
that Cloud Storage bucket.

00:24:15.060 --> 00:24:16.768
It can grab it and do
some stuff with it.

00:24:16.768 --> 00:24:18.760
You can also trigger
it from Pub/Sub.

00:24:18.760 --> 00:24:24.320
So one Cloud Function can
subscribe to a Pub/Sub topic.

00:24:24.320 --> 00:24:27.280
You can also monitor
a Firestore database.

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:28.980
So it can be
triggered from that.

00:24:28.980 --> 00:24:31.960
So data changes in the
Firestore database,

00:24:31.960 --> 00:24:37.630
and a Cloud Function can
be activated as well.

00:24:37.630 --> 00:24:42.968
So we, in our prototypes, we're
using Cloud Functions for,

00:24:42.968 --> 00:24:44.760
as the example he
showed, calling webhooks.

00:24:44.760 --> 00:24:46.930
So a Cloud Function
spins up, hits

00:24:46.930 --> 00:24:49.570
some other service and
some other cloud provider

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:51.880
or wherever.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:54.520
Cloud Functions can
easily read and write data

00:24:54.520 --> 00:24:59.470
to a Firestore database,
Cloud SQL, BigQuery.

00:24:59.470 --> 00:25:01.200
And of course, you
can send data out.

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.510
Pub/Sub, you can publish
from Cloud Function

00:25:04.510 --> 00:25:06.430
to an external
service or obviously

00:25:06.430 --> 00:25:08.610
another Cloud Function.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.420
You can see from the
previous examples

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:12.540
that we use Cloud Functions
all over the place.

00:25:16.097 --> 00:25:17.680
Next I'm going to
talk about BigQuery.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:19.210
So BigQuery, I love BigQuery.

00:25:19.210 --> 00:25:20.710
BigQuery is awesome.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:23.200
If you haven't used BigQuery,
you should use BigQuery.

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:25.930
So one point I like to
make about BigQuery.

00:25:25.930 --> 00:25:28.780
You don't need to have
big data to use BigQuery.

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:31.870
So you can have just a
little data and BigQuery.

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:34.750
So it's great, because
who wants to spin up

00:25:34.750 --> 00:25:38.095
a SQL server if you have a
task you want to complete.

00:25:38.095 --> 00:25:40.470
You need someplace to store
some data and query it later.

00:25:40.470 --> 00:25:41.820
BigQuery will work.

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:45.060
You could have 100 rows.

00:25:45.060 --> 00:25:51.010
Basically to set up
BigQuery, you go into the UI,

00:25:51.010 --> 00:25:53.020
or you could do it via
a command line or API.

00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:54.998
You create a data set.

00:25:54.998 --> 00:25:56.290
Then you load data into tables.

00:25:56.290 --> 00:25:58.430
That's it.

00:25:58.430 --> 00:26:01.890
And like many of
the GPC services,

00:26:01.890 --> 00:26:03.170
it's pay for what you use.

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:05.920
So for BigQuery, you
pay to query the data.

00:26:05.920 --> 00:26:08.140
You get a lot of--

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:09.900
there's a lot of--
you'll see later.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:11.590
There's a free tier for that.

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:16.060
But it's relatively
inexpensive, especially

00:26:16.060 --> 00:26:18.030
with small data sets.

00:26:18.030 --> 00:26:21.265
And then you pay a tiny
bit for data storage.

00:26:21.265 --> 00:26:23.560
I'm talking pennies per gig.

00:26:23.560 --> 00:26:26.800
And there's very little
configuration for a BigQuery.

00:26:26.800 --> 00:26:29.590
So you want to make a table,
you define the table schema.

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:32.920
And then you create a table,
and you load data into it.

00:26:32.920 --> 00:26:35.590
So a couple of ways to
load data into BigQuery.

00:26:35.590 --> 00:26:37.120
You can stream the data in.

00:26:37.120 --> 00:26:39.550
So BigQuery supports
streaming at--

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:44.260
you can stream up to 100,000
rows per second into BigQuery.

00:26:44.260 --> 00:26:48.140
So, obviously, it's
very performant.

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:49.690
You could also
load via batch job.

00:26:49.690 --> 00:26:53.350
So think about taking a
JSON file or a CSV file,

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.540
put it into Cloud Storage.

00:26:55.540 --> 00:26:56.930
Point at BigQuery.

00:26:56.930 --> 00:27:01.720
It'll ingest it and put
it in a table for you.

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:05.470
You query your data out of
BigQuery using standard SQL.

00:27:05.470 --> 00:27:07.550
You can use common VI tools.

00:27:07.550 --> 00:27:09.260
An example we had was Tableau.

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:11.500
But it works-- if you
walked through here,

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:14.098
you've surely seen 20
different tools that you

00:27:14.098 --> 00:27:15.140
can query your data with.

00:27:15.140 --> 00:27:19.600
You know, Looker, Data Studio.

00:27:19.600 --> 00:27:25.255
You can connect-- you
can use Cloud Datalab.

00:27:25.255 --> 00:27:26.710
It connects directly
to BigQuery.

00:27:26.710 --> 00:27:30.970
So data scientists,
data engineers

00:27:30.970 --> 00:27:35.050
spin up an IPython notebook,
is what Cloud Datalab is.

00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:37.520
And that'll connect directly
to BigQuery as well.

00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:40.420
So one thing you
wouldn't use BigQuery for

00:27:40.420 --> 00:27:42.400
is to store your
data if you're going

00:27:42.400 --> 00:27:44.950
to try and drive a website,
like a real-time dashboard.

00:27:44.950 --> 00:27:48.095
So one thing you'll learn when
you do a query, it can take--

00:27:48.095 --> 00:27:49.720
it's going to take
a couple of seconds.

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:54.900
Doesn't matter if you have
20 rows or 20,000 rows.

00:27:54.900 --> 00:27:56.980
It's a couple of seconds
to query that data.

00:27:56.980 --> 00:27:58.960
So you wouldn't want to
wait to populate a web

00:27:58.960 --> 00:28:00.640
page with data in BigQuery.

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:03.430
You want to run a query
and bring back a report.

00:28:07.730 --> 00:28:09.490
So where would I
store my data if we

00:28:09.490 --> 00:28:12.620
wanted to have it
available on a web page?

00:28:12.620 --> 00:28:14.440
So we use Cloud Firestore a lot.

00:28:14.440 --> 00:28:16.540
Works well with Cloud Functions.

00:28:16.540 --> 00:28:18.628
It's all integrated.

00:28:18.628 --> 00:28:19.670
It's a document database.

00:28:19.670 --> 00:28:22.390
So you're storing key
value pairs, NoSQL.

00:28:22.390 --> 00:28:26.110
So we'll commonly use that
to store configuration,

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:30.110
application state,
stuff like that.

00:28:30.110 --> 00:28:31.870
So it supports
real-time updating too.

00:28:31.870 --> 00:28:36.040
So a web developer can
create a dashboard.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:39.580
And with very little
effort, you can have it

00:28:39.580 --> 00:28:42.760
so that if a value
updates in Firestore,

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.130
it just automatically
updates in your dashboard.

00:28:45.130 --> 00:28:47.020
So you're looking
at a web page, you

00:28:47.020 --> 00:28:50.020
don't have to do
anything to pull data.

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:52.780
It automatically
shows updated value.

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:53.530
So we'll use that.

00:28:53.530 --> 00:28:55.360
And we're building
dashboards showing

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:58.810
application state or status
or counters, things like that.

00:28:58.810 --> 00:29:00.730
We'll use Firestore for that.

00:29:00.730 --> 00:29:04.910
You can back up your Firestore
data to Cloud Storage as well.

00:29:04.910 --> 00:29:07.820
So Google added
support for that.

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:11.530
So from there, once you have
your Firestone database,

00:29:11.530 --> 00:29:14.010
you can back up the
whole thing, pretty much.

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:16.120
You can restore it
back into Firestore.

00:29:16.120 --> 00:29:20.000
So you could use that
for disaster recovery.

00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:22.672
So not necessarily
that something crashed

00:29:22.672 --> 00:29:23.630
and you lost your data.

00:29:23.630 --> 00:29:26.670
But more like if somebody--
you overwrote your data

00:29:26.670 --> 00:29:29.660
or you deleted it, you maybe
wanted a copy to restore to.

00:29:29.660 --> 00:29:31.160
You can do that.

00:29:31.160 --> 00:29:33.837
You can also export
that backup to BigQuery.

00:29:33.837 --> 00:29:35.420
And we've done that
quite a few times.

00:29:35.420 --> 00:29:37.270
So if you have all
this data in Firestore,

00:29:37.270 --> 00:29:40.990
you can't really point an
analytics tool at Firestore.

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:42.680
But you can obviously
do it to BigQuery.

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:47.560
So Google has made a feature
of the Firestore backup

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.270
is that you can restore
that directly to BigQuery

00:29:50.270 --> 00:29:51.550
and do your analytics on it.

00:29:51.550 --> 00:29:53.383
And we've got customers
that are doing that.

00:29:56.700 --> 00:30:00.470
So another thing we use
a lot, Cloud Storage.

00:30:00.470 --> 00:30:03.800
Obviously, Cloud
Storage is used--

00:30:03.800 --> 00:30:05.240
most people know about that.

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:08.390
You can store anything in Cloud
Storage as long as it's a file.

00:30:08.390 --> 00:30:11.415
So a couple of things
about Cloud Storage

00:30:11.415 --> 00:30:12.290
that are interesting.

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:13.580
So it's virtually unlimited.

00:30:13.580 --> 00:30:15.038
Store as much as
you want in there.

00:30:15.038 --> 00:30:16.370
You just pay for what you use.

00:30:16.370 --> 00:30:19.370
Again, it's pennies
per gigabyte.

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:22.103
There is a 5 terabyte
file size limit, though.

00:30:22.103 --> 00:30:24.020
So if you have a file
bigger than 5 terabytes,

00:30:24.020 --> 00:30:26.220
you're going to
have to cut it up.

00:30:26.220 --> 00:30:28.430
It's got 11 nines of durability.

00:30:28.430 --> 00:30:30.670
So you're not going to
lose your files that

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:32.510
are in Cloud Storage.

00:30:32.510 --> 00:30:34.520
And there are many tools
for managing your data

00:30:34.520 --> 00:30:35.250
in Cloud Storage.

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:40.490
So you can use their client
libraries, command line tools,

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:42.050
the Cloud console.

00:30:42.050 --> 00:30:43.460
So when you get
back home and you

00:30:43.460 --> 00:30:45.865
start spinning up your
projects in the Cloud console,

00:30:45.865 --> 00:30:47.240
you can upload
and download files

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:51.690
directly out of Cloud Storage
using the web UI as well.

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:54.170
There are multiple storage
classes in Cloud Storage.

00:30:54.170 --> 00:30:57.500
So you may have heard
of the regional buckets,

00:30:57.500 --> 00:30:58.370
multiregional.

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:03.448
So when you create a bucket,
you decide what type of bucket

00:31:03.448 --> 00:31:05.240
you want, what you want
your storage to be.

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:09.110
So if you pick a
multiregional bucket,

00:31:09.110 --> 00:31:12.440
then you store data there,
Google spreads that data out,

00:31:12.440 --> 00:31:15.620
stores it, bunch of
POPs all over the place,

00:31:15.620 --> 00:31:17.763
and puts the data
closer to your users.

00:31:17.763 --> 00:31:19.430
So if you've got users
downloading data,

00:31:19.430 --> 00:31:21.777
there'll be less latency there.

00:31:21.777 --> 00:31:24.110
You wouldn't necessarily want
to do that if you're doing

00:31:24.110 --> 00:31:25.520
analytics workloads, though.

00:31:25.520 --> 00:31:28.040
So they also have
a regional bucket.

00:31:28.040 --> 00:31:30.470
So you can imagine if
you have a ton of data

00:31:30.470 --> 00:31:33.620
you need to process,
you would build your app

00:31:33.620 --> 00:31:38.210
to run and deploy it
to, like the US central.

00:31:38.210 --> 00:31:42.230
Well, you can deploy your
bucket, data from your bucket,

00:31:42.230 --> 00:31:43.310
also in US central.

00:31:46.370 --> 00:31:47.370
So it can download fast.

00:31:47.370 --> 00:31:50.000
And it costs a little less
when you do regional as well.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:51.880
So they also have
Nearline, Coldline.

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:53.580
And you've probably
heard of those.

00:31:53.580 --> 00:31:56.540
That's for less
frequently accessed data.

00:31:56.540 --> 00:31:58.988
We typically wouldn't
use that for a prototype.

00:31:58.988 --> 00:32:00.530
You usually store
in that for backup.

00:32:12.420 --> 00:32:15.560
So talk a minute
about Cloud SQL.

00:32:15.560 --> 00:32:16.850
I like Cloud SQL.

00:32:16.850 --> 00:32:20.030
It's not as easy to
set up as BigQuery.

00:32:20.030 --> 00:32:21.830
But it's certainly
simple enough.

00:32:21.830 --> 00:32:24.860
So we don't use it as much
for prototyping because it's

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:26.420
more configuration.

00:32:26.420 --> 00:32:29.510
It's still a great option
to store your data.

00:32:29.510 --> 00:32:32.270
Currently, Cloud SQL
supports MySQL and Postgres.

00:32:32.270 --> 00:32:34.310
You can have a
SQL server running

00:32:34.310 --> 00:32:35.850
in five clicks in the console.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:38.210
You can go into the
GCP Cloud console.

00:32:38.210 --> 00:32:40.400
Five clicks later, you've
got a SQL server running.

00:32:43.112 --> 00:32:44.570
Supports all the
stuff you'd expect

00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:46.520
from a database service--

00:32:46.520 --> 00:32:49.790
high availability,
failover, automated backups,

00:32:49.790 --> 00:32:53.290
point in time recovery,
auto storage increase.

00:32:53.290 --> 00:32:58.270
And you can have up to
10 terabytes of storage.

00:32:58.270 --> 00:33:00.020
You connect to it just
like any other SQL.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:03.890
So if you've got
an app that needs

00:33:03.890 --> 00:33:06.562
to communicate with a
MySQL or Postgres server,

00:33:06.562 --> 00:33:09.020
you connect to it the same way
if it's running on Cloud SQL

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:10.340
or if it runs on
a virtual machine

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:11.965
or if it's running
in your data center.

00:33:20.520 --> 00:33:25.560
So another thing that we've been
using a lot is Cloud Dataflow.

00:33:25.560 --> 00:33:29.460
So Cloud Dataflow is a
fully managed service.

00:33:29.460 --> 00:33:32.320
It's supports stream
and batch processing.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:34.890
So one of the ideas
with Cloud Dataflow

00:33:34.890 --> 00:33:38.310
is that if you write your app
correctly in Cloud Dataflow,

00:33:38.310 --> 00:33:41.550
you can use the same logic
whether it's streaming data in

00:33:41.550 --> 00:33:43.020
or you're doing batch.

00:33:43.020 --> 00:33:43.890
So it's server-less.

00:33:43.890 --> 00:33:45.790
It's auto-scaling.

00:33:45.790 --> 00:33:50.190
So the idea with Cloud
Dataflow is you submit a job.

00:33:50.190 --> 00:33:52.420
It'll spin up as many
nodes as it needs to.

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:54.200
It'll work until the job's done.

00:33:54.200 --> 00:33:56.020
And then it'll spin them down.

00:33:56.020 --> 00:33:58.383
If you're doing streaming,
it obviously won't do that.

00:33:58.383 --> 00:34:00.300
It'll keep your streaming
job running forever,

00:34:00.300 --> 00:34:02.790
or until you kill it.

00:34:02.790 --> 00:34:06.900
So common pattern
for Cloud Dataflow

00:34:06.900 --> 00:34:11.159
is to write data
out to BigQuery.

00:34:11.159 --> 00:34:13.400
So we showed that previously.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:16.870
So you can also write data out
to Cloud Storage or Pub/Sub.

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:23.100
And with the example
that we had earlier,

00:34:23.100 --> 00:34:25.980
we were using Dataflow to
do data redaction, data

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:26.739
enrichment.

00:34:26.739 --> 00:34:30.179
So, in that example,
Dataflow was reading

00:34:30.179 --> 00:34:31.739
data out of a BigQuery table.

00:34:31.739 --> 00:34:35.100
It was reading flat files
out of Cloud Storage,

00:34:35.100 --> 00:34:38.940
combining that data, and then
redacting it and pushing it

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:41.739
back into a different
BigQuery table.

00:34:41.739 --> 00:34:45.389
And then, from there, the
guys could do their analytics

00:34:45.389 --> 00:34:46.380
on that clean data.

00:34:50.616 --> 00:34:52.449
So Firebase Hosting is
another thing we use.

00:34:52.449 --> 00:34:53.699
That's pretty straightforward.

00:34:53.699 --> 00:34:55.210
All it is is static files.

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:59.020
So if we're building
a dashboard or there's

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:02.590
some web component
to our prototype

00:35:02.590 --> 00:35:04.200
we're building for
somebody, you stick

00:35:04.200 --> 00:35:07.240
your HTML, JavaScript,
CSS, all that stuff

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:09.150
can go in a Firebase Hosting.

00:35:09.150 --> 00:35:11.980
It's really easy
to use, supports

00:35:11.980 --> 00:35:16.170
custom domains, all that stuff.

00:35:16.170 --> 00:35:19.520
Another thing we're using
is Firebase Authentication.

00:35:19.520 --> 00:35:21.850
So authentication isn't easy.

00:35:21.850 --> 00:35:24.440
Firebase Authentication
makes it a little easier.

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:26.980
So it's quick and easy
to set up, anyway.

00:35:26.980 --> 00:35:30.190
And it's a secure way
to add auth to your app.

00:35:30.190 --> 00:35:32.840
So when you're doing a
prototype, a lot of times

00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:35.950
you don't want to spend a
ton of time building roles

00:35:35.950 --> 00:35:38.590
and permissions and
all the authentication

00:35:38.590 --> 00:35:41.140
that you would have
in a full-blown app.

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:45.440
Firebase makes it a
lot easier to do that.

00:35:45.440 --> 00:35:49.417
In our case, instead of doing--
so with Firebase, you can do,

00:35:49.417 --> 00:35:51.250
there's I don't know
how many different ways

00:35:51.250 --> 00:35:54.660
to do authentication and
social and all this stuff.

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:56.620
What we usually do for
a prototype is we'll

00:35:56.620 --> 00:35:58.780
just enable username password.

00:35:58.780 --> 00:36:00.220
So it's email
password, actually.

00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:03.820
So you can go into the console
and put emails and passwords

00:36:03.820 --> 00:36:04.360
in.

00:36:04.360 --> 00:36:06.870
And Firebase takes care of that.

00:36:06.870 --> 00:36:10.360
So they're hashing it and
encrypting and storing it.

00:36:10.360 --> 00:36:13.690
Then we use APIs to authenticate
a web app with that.

00:36:13.690 --> 00:36:16.990
And, in a lot of cases, we'll
give that Firebase console--

00:36:16.990 --> 00:36:18.953
since we're building
these for our customers,

00:36:18.953 --> 00:36:20.620
we'll give that project
to the customer.

00:36:20.620 --> 00:36:22.510
And the customer can
log into the console

00:36:22.510 --> 00:36:23.930
and add their own users to it.

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:27.419
So they've got a UI built in
to do that right in Firebase.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:35.010
So back to the list.

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:37.560
I obviously didn't go
through all 50 of them.

00:36:37.560 --> 00:36:39.240
I just scratched the surface.

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.320
I didn't get in a ton of detail.

00:36:41.320 --> 00:36:43.872
There's so much-- we could
spend a whole week talking

00:36:43.872 --> 00:36:44.580
about this stuff.

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:51.570
But we will use a lot
of these other services

00:36:51.570 --> 00:36:53.848
as well, obviously,
if it makes sense.

00:36:53.848 --> 00:36:56.140
There are a lot of different
ways to do the same thing.

00:36:56.140 --> 00:36:58.350
And for prototypes,
we try and find

00:36:58.350 --> 00:37:00.300
the most efficient,
quickest way to do it,

00:37:00.300 --> 00:37:02.970
simplest way to do it, so we're
not spending a lot of time

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:05.420
and a lot of money.

00:37:05.420 --> 00:37:06.400
It's a big list.

00:37:06.400 --> 00:37:09.000
So if you guys want to geek
out about any of these things

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:11.160
later, we can stop by our booth.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:15.340
I'll be glad to talk to you
about it as much as you want.

00:37:15.340 --> 00:37:19.020
So with that, I'm going to
hand it back over to Patrick,

00:37:19.020 --> 00:37:20.477
let him talk for a little bit.

00:37:20.477 --> 00:37:23.816
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:27.160 --> 00:37:28.330
PATRICK MECHURA: All right.

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:30.200
Before I move on to
this next slide here,

00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:31.510
one more question for you guys.

00:37:31.510 --> 00:37:32.580
Who likes free stuff?

00:37:36.200 --> 00:37:38.860
If you don't know about
GCP's free tier, you should.

00:37:38.860 --> 00:37:44.380
It's literally free--
hence, free tier.

00:37:44.380 --> 00:37:47.320
It's really helpful when you're
testing out a new technology.

00:37:47.320 --> 00:37:49.357
You don't have to spend
your own funds on it.

00:37:49.357 --> 00:37:51.940
It's a good way to play around
with some of their new services

00:37:51.940 --> 00:37:54.280
that they've
announced here today.

00:37:54.280 --> 00:37:58.360
If you land on
cloud.google.com/free,

00:37:58.360 --> 00:37:59.920
it'll show you a
list of services.

00:37:59.920 --> 00:38:02.045
And when I was throwing
this presentation together,

00:38:02.045 --> 00:38:03.190
that was the list.

00:38:03.190 --> 00:38:05.380
I can't promise that that
is still the same list.

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:07.845
Google reserves the
right to change that.

00:38:07.845 --> 00:38:08.720
But you can go there.

00:38:08.720 --> 00:38:11.220
It'll show you all the details
of what their free tier is

00:38:11.220 --> 00:38:13.405
and some of the caveats.

00:38:13.405 --> 00:38:15.280
But a lot of the services
Ray just touched on

00:38:15.280 --> 00:38:18.700
that we use in our prototypes
every day are on that list

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:19.210
right now.

00:38:19.210 --> 00:38:20.400
So you have Cloud Firestore.

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:24.940
You have Cloud Storage, Pub/Sub,
Cloud Functions, GKE, BigQuery.

00:38:24.940 --> 00:38:27.250
So you can get a lot of
value from the free tier

00:38:27.250 --> 00:38:28.400
if you're leveraging it.

00:38:31.350 --> 00:38:33.310
So a couple of
free tier examples

00:38:33.310 --> 00:38:35.780
that I like to touch
on, that I really find

00:38:35.780 --> 00:38:37.550
a lot of worth or value in.

00:38:37.550 --> 00:38:39.140
Cloud Functions is one of them.

00:38:39.140 --> 00:38:41.655
You get 2 million
invocations a month.

00:38:41.655 --> 00:38:43.310
That's a lot.

00:38:43.310 --> 00:38:46.640
There are limitations on compute
time and some network egress.

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:50.720
But 2 million invocations is
still nothing to scoff at.

00:38:50.720 --> 00:38:52.610
The other one would be BigQuery.

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:55.700
Obviously, we have a little
bit of a crush on BigQuery.

00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:59.440
But you can run a terabyte
of queries a month.

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:01.870
That's nothing to
scoff at either.

00:39:01.870 --> 00:39:03.620
10 gigabyte storage limit.

00:39:03.620 --> 00:39:07.880
Still, quite a bit of
data that you can query.

00:39:07.880 --> 00:39:10.600
So what's the point
in all of this?

00:39:10.600 --> 00:39:12.410
Leverage your free
tiers and leverage

00:39:12.410 --> 00:39:13.740
the cloud native services.

00:39:13.740 --> 00:39:15.490
All of the services
that Ray went over

00:39:15.490 --> 00:39:17.510
and that free tier that
I was just discussing,

00:39:17.510 --> 00:39:19.460
they're meant to make
your life easier,

00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:22.130
help you save money and
resource hours, something

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:25.370
that I'm sure all of you can
appreciate in your day to days.

00:39:25.370 --> 00:39:27.920
And also, it helps you
focus on some more strategic

00:39:27.920 --> 00:39:28.430
initiatives.

00:39:28.430 --> 00:39:30.138
If you're able to save
that time, and you

00:39:30.138 --> 00:39:32.633
start focusing on some
more innovative thoughts

00:39:32.633 --> 00:39:34.050
that you and your
team might have.

00:39:34.050 --> 00:39:36.008
So that's a lot better
than just focusing

00:39:36.008 --> 00:39:37.050
on keeping the lights on.

00:39:39.990 --> 00:39:40.680
All right.

00:39:40.680 --> 00:39:41.560
Last thing for today.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:43.630
Dos and don'ts.

00:39:43.630 --> 00:39:46.600
So we're consultants, obviously.

00:39:46.600 --> 00:39:48.100
We work with a lot
of organizations,

00:39:48.100 --> 00:39:52.630
and we get to see both the
good, the bad, and the ugly.

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:55.270
So some of these dos and don'ts
are going to seem like complete

00:39:55.270 --> 00:39:56.740
no-brainers.

00:39:56.740 --> 00:39:58.340
I wish they were.

00:39:58.340 --> 00:39:59.650
They're not always.

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:02.085
So if it is a no-brainer
for you, that's good.

00:40:02.085 --> 00:40:03.460
That means you're
doing it right.

00:40:03.460 --> 00:40:07.690
But just some things
to keep in mind.

00:40:07.690 --> 00:40:08.790
So the first do.

00:40:08.790 --> 00:40:12.450
Start with an idea
and formulate a plan.

00:40:12.450 --> 00:40:13.882
Simple, right?

00:40:13.882 --> 00:40:14.465
Of course.

00:40:14.465 --> 00:40:16.590
But it's really easy to
get excited about your idea

00:40:16.590 --> 00:40:18.515
and just jump straight into it.

00:40:18.515 --> 00:40:20.640
But if you're doing that,
you're probably not going

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:21.723
to end up very successful.

00:40:21.723 --> 00:40:22.848
You're going to waste time.

00:40:22.848 --> 00:40:24.450
You're going to
waste budget hours.

00:40:24.450 --> 00:40:25.560
No one wants that.

00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:27.810
So slow down a bit,
define your approach,

00:40:27.810 --> 00:40:30.190
figure out your key
use case requirements,

00:40:30.190 --> 00:40:33.420
and then take action.

00:40:33.420 --> 00:40:36.030
That said, don't
overcomplicate your approach.

00:40:36.030 --> 00:40:37.500
It's a PoC, or it's a prototype.

00:40:37.500 --> 00:40:39.570
It doesn't need
to be complicated.

00:40:39.570 --> 00:40:42.000
So just define it
at a high level.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:45.760
Define what is going
to look like success.

00:40:45.760 --> 00:40:52.880
So, speaking of success, do
define your success criteria.

00:40:52.880 --> 00:40:54.680
If you don't define
your success criteria,

00:40:54.680 --> 00:40:58.010
how are you going to brag about
being successful to your boss,

00:40:58.010 --> 00:40:59.792
to your colleagues, anybody?

00:40:59.792 --> 00:41:01.250
It also helps the
rest of your team

00:41:01.250 --> 00:41:03.060
stay focused on the end goal.

00:41:03.060 --> 00:41:04.920
If you don't have
that success criteria,

00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:08.748
how do they know what they
should be focusing on?

00:41:08.748 --> 00:41:10.790
One important thing around
that success criteria,

00:41:10.790 --> 00:41:12.890
it's not just functional.

00:41:12.890 --> 00:41:14.750
There is nonfunctional
requirements as well.

00:41:14.750 --> 00:41:16.340
Do you have performance
requirements?

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:17.832
Do you have security
requirements?

00:41:17.832 --> 00:41:19.790
Those are things that
you should be considering

00:41:19.790 --> 00:41:20.750
in these prototypes.

00:41:24.390 --> 00:41:28.220
But don't confuse a PoC or
your prototype with an MVP.

00:41:28.220 --> 00:41:31.350
So prototypes kind of land
in between PoCs and MVPs,

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:34.000
as far as I'm concerned.

00:41:34.000 --> 00:41:36.870
Your MVP is meant to be
a Market Viable Product.

00:41:36.870 --> 00:41:38.430
So a minimum viable product.

00:41:38.430 --> 00:41:41.310
But it's market ready.

00:41:41.310 --> 00:41:42.825
PoC is generally
internal-facing.

00:41:42.825 --> 00:41:44.700
You're trying to determine
feasibility of it,

00:41:44.700 --> 00:41:47.820
maybe test a new technology,
prove something out.

00:41:47.820 --> 00:41:49.830
So if their success
criteria are identical,

00:41:49.830 --> 00:41:51.400
you should probably
take a step back,

00:41:51.400 --> 00:41:52.980
think about why
they're identical,

00:41:52.980 --> 00:41:55.380
and figure out which one
you actually need to do.

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:58.290
If you're not proving out a
new concept or new technology,

00:41:58.290 --> 00:42:00.660
maybe you don't need
to start with a PoC.

00:42:00.660 --> 00:42:03.810
So either way, having the
right success criteria

00:42:03.810 --> 00:42:04.890
is key for the last one.

00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:06.570
I've already alluded to this.

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:08.903
Set expectations with
your key stakeholders.

00:42:08.903 --> 00:42:10.320
There's nothing
worse than getting

00:42:10.320 --> 00:42:13.835
to the end of a project,
presenting what you have.

00:42:13.835 --> 00:42:14.460
You're excited.

00:42:14.460 --> 00:42:16.765
You think it was a
smashing success.

00:42:16.765 --> 00:42:19.140
And your stakeholders have
this blank look on their face.

00:42:19.140 --> 00:42:19.848
They're confused.

00:42:19.848 --> 00:42:22.390
They're wondering where the
features they asked for were.

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:24.890
And it's because you didn't set
their expectations properly,

00:42:24.890 --> 00:42:25.900
or at least most likely.

00:42:25.900 --> 00:42:28.380
So, if you can talk with
them early and often,

00:42:28.380 --> 00:42:30.750
set those expectations on
what you're going to deliver,

00:42:30.750 --> 00:42:32.700
when you're going to
deliver it, that's

00:42:32.700 --> 00:42:34.150
going to help you
in the long run

00:42:34.150 --> 00:42:36.090
to get the reactions
that you want at the end.

00:42:38.940 --> 00:42:40.650
The other side of
that coin, don't

00:42:40.650 --> 00:42:42.910
be afraid of changing
course or strategy.

00:42:42.910 --> 00:42:44.410
I've seen it a
couple of times where

00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:47.012
a project owner,
a product owner,

00:42:47.012 --> 00:42:49.470
doesn't want to change their
course because they've already

00:42:49.470 --> 00:42:50.790
reported to the
stakeholders, this

00:42:50.790 --> 00:42:52.540
is how we're going to
do it, and we're not

00:42:52.540 --> 00:42:54.580
going to do it any other way.

00:42:54.580 --> 00:42:56.160
But they get down
that path, they

00:42:56.160 --> 00:42:58.080
realize it's not
a viable strategy.

00:42:58.080 --> 00:43:00.640
And they still continue
down that path.

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:02.300
You're wasting time and money.

00:43:02.300 --> 00:43:04.800
That's not good for you, and
it's not good for your company.

00:43:04.800 --> 00:43:06.675
What you really should
be doing at that point

00:43:06.675 --> 00:43:10.380
is pausing, reassessing
your other options,

00:43:10.380 --> 00:43:12.600
resetting expectations
with your stakeholders,

00:43:12.600 --> 00:43:15.530
and changing your course.

00:43:15.530 --> 00:43:18.000
The whole point of
a PoC or prototype

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:20.070
is to determine feasibility.

00:43:20.070 --> 00:43:22.650
So if you find out
that it's not feasible,

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:26.855
there's no point in
continuing down that path.

00:43:26.855 --> 00:43:30.410
That leads to the
last point of the day.

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:34.100
Have a plan, communicate
it, and take action.

00:43:34.100 --> 00:43:36.290
The last one is key as well.

00:43:36.290 --> 00:43:37.160
Take action.

00:43:37.160 --> 00:43:39.390
A lot of projects fail
because they haven't started.

00:43:39.390 --> 00:43:42.634
So don't forget that last one.

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:47.680
So this is about
wrapping it up for us.

00:43:47.680 --> 00:43:49.530
Just a couple of key points.

00:43:49.530 --> 00:43:50.780
We've already touched on this.

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:52.970
But if you learn nothing
else from us today,

00:43:52.970 --> 00:43:55.403
hopefully these can
stick in your head.

00:43:55.403 --> 00:43:56.570
Number one, unique problems.

00:43:56.570 --> 00:43:58.195
They don't have to
be unique solutions.

00:43:58.195 --> 00:43:59.690
Use reference architectures.

00:43:59.690 --> 00:44:01.220
They're your friend.

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:03.780
Leverage the free tiers
and cloud native services

00:44:03.780 --> 00:44:07.380
to help you save money and time.

00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:10.400
And then have a plan,
communicate that criteria,

00:44:10.400 --> 00:44:13.040
and take action.

00:44:13.040 --> 00:44:16.390
[APPLAUSE]

